WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.790
It is great to welcome to the program today.

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:09.509
Phil Gursky, who's a 30 year Canadian intelligence
veteran, author of five books on terrorism

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:12.809
and also host of the podcast and intelligent
look at Terrorism.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:14.559
Phil, it's great to have you on today.

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:15.900
Hey, it's great to be here, David.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.099
Thanks for inviting me on the show.

00:00:18.099 --> 00:00:19.529
So, I mean, where to start, right?

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:26.210
I guess the war on terror has now been waged
for decades.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.210
How is it going?

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.119
Are we winning it yet?

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:31.480
No, we're not.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.960
And, you know, you referenced the fact that
I've written five books.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.550
My fourth book was actually entitled An End
to the War on Terrorism.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:39.870
I hate the term.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.510
I've hated it since day one.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.920
As you noted in your introduction, I work
in security intelligence for that.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.000
So for the Canadian security intelligence
service, kind of like an FBI, CIA hybrid,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.739
we looked at homegrown plots, Canadians traveling
to join al-Qaeda later, ISIS, et cetera.

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:55.410
Al-Shabaab in Somalia.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.260
And yet we're framing this through the military.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:02.870
And the problem is as as American academic
said very wisely after 9/11, he says whatever

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:07.960
you do, don't declare war on a common now
because it never ends.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.340
Think of the war on drugs in your country.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.700
You think of the war on poverty.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.590
You think on the war on any common man, because
you said common nouns don't surrender, whereas

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.390
proper nouns do.

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.840
So you asked me, how's it going?

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.979
It's going well and not so well.

00:01:20.979 --> 00:01:25.400
Well, in a sense that in your country and
mine, we're not seeing terrorist attacks on

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.400
a regular basis.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.740
Let's face it, that's that's true.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:32.159
And yet, if you look at the news on any given
day, David, which I do as a as a regular course,

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:33.159
you know, I'm retired.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.439
I'm still involved in this business every
day in Afghanistan, Somalia or Nigeria or

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:39.149
Iraq or Syria.

00:01:39.149 --> 00:01:43.380
There's an attack and several cases, many
attacks per day in those countries.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.420
So it's going both well and not so well, depending
on your perspective, I guess, here in the

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.159
United States.

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:55.020
There is an interesting report from the Anti
Defamation League, which I've referenced before,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:01.270
which looked at a politically based domestic
terror killings, so to speak.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.240
And they are overwhelmingly right wing.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:11.820
The numbers, as I recall, I think in twenty
nineteen were 76 percent right wing, 20 percent

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:15.770
Islamic extremism, and then four percent left
or sort of miscellaneous.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:22.550
Now, from a logistical perspective, when I
hear that someone believes their religion

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:26.550
should be the law of the land and is willing
to kill for those reasons.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.540
To me, that sounds right wing.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.890
So when I think of Islamic extremism, it's
often framed as left wing.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:36.800
It sounds really right wing to me in the intelligence
community.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.360
Is there a position on that, on the characterization
of Islamic violence?

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.850
Wow, I've never thought of it in that term
before.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:48.540
We've certainly looked at Islamist extremism
as our number one threat.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.950
We had several plots in the 2000s and 2010s.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.650
Several Hunter Canadians want to join ISIS,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:57.800
We had a small right wing desk, which frankly
wasn't very busy.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.020
When I retired in 2015, I'm sure it's busier
right now.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.030
I would push back a little bit on the stats.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.250
It's true that in your country, obviously,
the far right is about two thirds or three

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:10.110
quarters of the incidents around the world
is still 95 percent Islamist extremism.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.260
If you look at the numbers across the world,
no denial whatsoever.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.780
Yeah, yeah.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:16.450
The way you framed it is really interesting.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:17.450
So I did write a book.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:19.650
My last book was entitled When Religions Kill.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.920
And I looked at how every religion has within
it an extreme element that justifies killing

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.140
in the name of God, Judaism, Christianity,
even Buddhism.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.140
My God.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.310
Buddhist extremists should be an oxymoron.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:34.120
And yet there are Buddhist terrorists in Sri
Lanka, in Thailand and Myanmar.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:37.670
I guess you would say that Islamist extremists
are conservative.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:39.160
They're definitely very intolerant.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:43.440
The view of Islam that they try to force on
people is exclusionary.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.980
Essentially, it's my way or the highway.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:51.260
Bernard Lewis, the American academic academic,
said very famously after 9/11, it's like it's

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:52.270
I'm right.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:53.400
You're wrong.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:54.400
Go to hell.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.900
That's the way these people see the world.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:57.150
So I guess some perspective.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.380
We would look at it as right wing.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:03.270
But from my experience working in a case in
Intelligence Committee, it was its own separate

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:07.290
task and it was the lion's share of the counterterrorism
work for the better part of the 20 years you

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.210
just referred to in the post 9/11 period.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:15.970
When we think about the radicalization of
individuals, when I've interviewed domestic

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:21.900
extremists in the United States like former
KKK and white nationalists and others almost

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:29.200
exclusively, there is a story of there was
a lack of empathy and support in their lives

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.250
which led them vulnerable to sort of join
up with whoever paid them attention and accept

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:41.430
the orthodoxy that they presented in in international
terror and the work that you did.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.930
Is it a similar pattern?

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.760
What leads to the radicalization of an individual?

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:46.760
Wow.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:47.950
How much time do we have?

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:51.430
I mean, my very first book, The Threat, from
what they looked at it and I was based on

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:53.380
key cases, I worked in Canada.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:55.600
Unclassified information, obviously.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:56.900
But we found the opposite, David.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.740
We found that these people were run of the
mill average Canadians.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:02.210
They were average from a socio economic status.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:04.550
They were average from an educational background.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:06.840
They were average from a family background.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:08.790
They were average, insofar as we could tell.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.690
We're not psychologists from a mental health
perspective.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:16.390
There was nothing that sort of divided them
or separated them from the general Canadian

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:17.390
populace.

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:20.970
And I've seen the same stats for the United
States as well in terms of ism extremism.

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:24.130
I do think that we look at the far right KKK.

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:29.580
You know, the boogaloo boys, you might see
that kind of a bit of a tendency towards the

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:30.600
down and out in life.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.810
You know, you often hear the term marginalized.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:39.760
You hear the term not making ends meet, you
know, failing and education, failing at relationships,

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:41.240
failing at that work.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.260
We'll see you down in the ISM aside.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:44.610
But, of course, you have exceptions.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.250
You look at a country like France.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:47.250
Yeah.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:51.420
And a lot of the people who join ISIS and
al-Qaida in France are on the lower socio

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:52.880
economic spectrum.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:57.350
But I would argue you look at French Muslim
society in general and it's generally speaking,

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:58.350
gets on the lower.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.740
You know, your country and my country are
very different.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:03.040
Muslims in our country have tend to tend to
do very, very well.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:04.840
They tend to be highly educated.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:08.550
They tend to be have good jobs, solid families,
et cetera.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.980
In Europe, that's not the case for all kinds
of historical reasons.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.970
Bottom line, David, is that there's no profit
profile to any of this.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:17.630
And people that try to profile get into trouble
because we're trying to profile.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:18.860
You run into two problems.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:19.860
Yes.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:21.400
One's what to call a false positive.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:26.060
If you think that all jihadis are all right
wing guys are X and somebodies is X and is

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.060
not a jihadi.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:28.060
That's a problem.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:32.130
And then if you say that no jihadis are well
educated or no KKK or well-educated, the first

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.840
guy with a PD you don't look at because he
doesn't fit your your profile.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.300
So when we were at work that can skirt intelligence
service, we didn't have a profile.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.590
We looked at activity.

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:45.530
We looked at, you know, online statements
which we had warrants to intercept.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:49.680
We looked at what they said and what they
did, not who they were, where they came from

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.410
or what their particular circumstances were.

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:55.710
And I think that was a that was successful
for us when it comes to the leaders of terrorist

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:58.419
organizations over the last 20 years.

00:06:58.419 --> 00:07:03.610
We've all seen endless headlines of the number
two was killed, the number three.

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:04.610
You know what?

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:08.670
Whatever how how how important is that to
actually fighting terror?

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:11.889
And I'll make the question sort of a two part
question.

00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:18.550
One, if what we're fighting is an ideology,
is the ideology affected by killing an individual?

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:19.700
Is it sometimes?

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:20.790
Sometimes yes, sometimes no.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:27.060
OK, and then number two, even from the operational
standpoint, how effective is it to kill the

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:28.910
number one, the number two, the number three
guy?

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:30.930
I'm going to give you an answer.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.490
I'm going to like it depends.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:33.669
OK, so.

00:07:33.669 --> 00:07:38.090
So in some groups, it's all about the leader
of a group like Shining Path.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:39.910
So that'll do it also in Peru.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:44.540
When Guzman was taken by the Peruvians back
in the early 90s, the group never really recovered.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:47.590
They're still around, but they're a shadow
of their former selves.

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:48.590
You look at al-Qaida.

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:49.660
Bin Laden is gone now.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.150
Ayman al-Zawahiri can't hold a candle to bin
Laden.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:55.880
For my current charismatic perspective, he's
the he's like watching paint dry.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:57.639
And, you know, al-Qaida is still around.

00:07:57.639 --> 00:07:59.699
We've got Baghdadi, right, a couple of years.

00:07:59.699 --> 00:08:00.699
You guys got Baghdadi.

00:08:00.699 --> 00:08:03.520
ISIS is just a stronger force trauma than
it ever was.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:07.610
I better cases maybe the PKK in Turkey, the
Kurdish militant group.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:10.180
The Turks count Capstick Abdullah oculi back
in 1994.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:15.460
He's been in prison on an island near Turkey
for the better part of over 20 years.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:19.100
The cake, the PKK is carrying out attacks
on a weekly basis in eastern Turkey.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:24.301
So the short answer is it depends on the nature
of the group and the control of what the leader

00:08:24.301 --> 00:08:27.199
has against within that group.

00:08:27.199 --> 00:08:31.730
The second part of your question is, look,
it's never a bad thing to take out a major

00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:32.730
terrorist.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.080
We always used to say a dead terrorist is
a good terrorist.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:36.900
You don't have to investigate them anymore.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:38.270
You don't worry about him.

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:40.530
He can't exert his influence, et cetera, et
cetera.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:44.960
Now, we can argue about rule of law and whether
or not extraterritorial assassinations are

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:45.960
legal.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:46.960
That's a whole other discussion.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:48.960
So taking guys out is never a bad thing.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.950
The problem is it rarely, if ever, means the
end.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.030
And I'm I'm tired of reading headlines.

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:58.220
You know, we've got so and so this is the
end of group number X, X, Y or Z.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:03.410
It never is or very rarely is, because, as
you mentioned, is the ideology that's important,

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:04.410
not the individual.

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:09.200
The individual can be charismatic, attract
people, retain people, inspire people.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.350
But charismatic people are sometimes a dime
a dozen.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:12.770
It's the ideology that matters.

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:14.350
That's what you have to undermine.

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:15.760
And that's a lot more difficult to carry.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:16.880
You can't cure a drone strike.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:18.520
It gets an ideology, unfortunately.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:19.790
I want to get back to that.

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:24.600
But because you mentioned, you know, the question
as to the legality of extraterritorial killings

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.920
of suspected terrorists in the work you did.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:33.060
Did that play at all in in your work or is
that sort of like that was handled?

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:36.330
The legal questions were are handled somewhere
else?

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:37.330
Absolutely.

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:40.490
So we're we're essentially we were certainly
a domestic intelligence service, but we had

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:44.550
an international remit because Canadians were
fighting with the Taliban and al-Qaida, things

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:45.550
like that.

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:48.290
We had lawyers, obviously, that were able
to guide us.

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:52.089
But we follow the law in a sense that we went
to court to get warrants to intercept people

00:09:52.089 --> 00:09:53.089
communications.

00:09:53.089 --> 00:09:54.089
You have to do that.

00:09:54.089 --> 00:09:57.820
You can't you don't follow somebody out on
Twitter or intercept it because that's your

00:09:57.820 --> 00:09:59.220
legal in Canada.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:03.480
So from our perspective, we provided intelligence
and you provided to decision makers.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.230
Now, sometimes the decision makers by be in
the Canadian military or the U.S. military,

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:10.820
and they will decide to do with that intelligence,
whatever that their mandate says that they

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:12.060
can do.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:16.600
I didn't lose sleep at night personally about
my intelligence being used in a way that I

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:21.870
might not necessarily agree with for my own
ethical perspective, because, first of all,

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:23.750
a cat has a pretty small player, right?

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:27.900
We're not carrying out drone strikes that
you guys are in Yemen and Afghanistan and

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:29.240
Somalia.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:33.700
So if my perspective wasn't as important,
I do think there's a bigger conversation around

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:34.700
this.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:37.170
And I know that when Entry-Level Awlaki, who
is the American who was killed in Yemen a

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:41.040
couple of years ago, that raised a real kerfuffle
at your country because he was an American

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.140
citizen and didn't get his day in court.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:44.140
Right.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:45.880
He was to to refute the charges against them.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:49.240
I didn't lose sleep over Awlaki because he
was a major instigator.

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:52.430
He was a major charismatic leader of al-Qaida
in the Arabian Peninsula.

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:56.500
There wasn't a single Canadia that didn't
follow his writings or his videos online.

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:57.700
So he had disappeared.

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:00.090
It was it was a good, good news day for us.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.150
But on the other side of the coin, I do understand
the legal ramifications of it.

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:06.790
But when you work in intelligence, you provide
the best information you can to those that

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:07.790
can use it.

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:09.330
And you do so to the best of your ability.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:10.330
OK.

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:16.690
So now that that being kind of framed in terms
of your work, when we think about what does

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:22.180
work to root out an ideology, I mean, is it
one?

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:28.180
One area that doesn't seem to be discussed
enough is sort of the economic situation in

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:29.630
the source countries.

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:33.779
And when we talk about undocumented immigration
here in the United States, you know, the current

00:11:33.779 --> 00:11:37.630
administration talks about walls and they
talk about, you know, jail and all this stuff.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:42.260
If the economic circumstances were better
in the source countries, the demand for trying

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:46.070
to come to the United States illegally would
decrease dramatically.

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:53.709
Ken, does that apply to thinking about the
ideologies that rise and the way in which

00:11:53.709 --> 00:11:55.029
people can be injured?

00:11:55.029 --> 00:12:00.830
Dr. Unaided, can that be separated from the
context in which these countries are existing?

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:04.200
You're asking really tough questions for me
on Canada Day.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:05.200
That's a tough one.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:10.750
Again, it depends, because if you look at
9/11, for example, 15 of the 19 hijackers

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:13.860
that attacked New York and Washington were
rich Saudi individuals.

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:16.630
They didn't have any socio economic problems.

00:12:16.630 --> 00:12:22.010
I do think that it obviously makes sense from
a humanitarian and global stability perspective

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:28.070
to make sure that every country is as economically
